<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sénat conservateur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1799">1799</a>–<a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Conservative Senate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sénat conservateur was the senate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> under the Consulate and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">First Empire</a>, created by the constitution of 1799. Appointed and docile for most of its life, it endorsed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s rule, but in April 1814, with the allies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, it proclaimed his deposition and called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a> to the throne. It was replaced by the Chamber of Peers under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> Restoration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/senat-conservateur.jpg" alt="Sénat conservateur" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kilom691 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M%C3%A9daille_en_vermeil_S%C3%A9nat_conservateur_(Constitution_de_l%27an_VIII)_sans_txt.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Constitution of 22 frimaire year VIII (13 December 1799), drawn up under the direct influence of First Consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coup-of-18-brumaire">Coup of 18 Brumaire</a>, was the first to recreate a senate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Charged with watching over the survival of the constitution, the Sénat sat beside the Tribunat and the Corps législatif as one of the three major assemblies of the Consulate, though it did not exercise ordinary legislative powers.</p><p>It was to have 60 permanent members aged at least 40, with two more added each year for ten years. There were no elections. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-joseph-sieyes">Sieyès</a> and Roger Ducos, the outgoing consuls, became members by right and, with the new consuls Cambacérès and Lebrun, chose 29 others; this majority picked the rest. Thereafter the Senate filled vacancies itself from three candidates put forward by the First Consul, the Tribunat and the Corps législatif. It met, not in public, at the Luxembourg Palace, and its first members included Monge, Lagrange, Berthollet, Cabanis, the explorer Bougainville and the painter Vien.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A revision of the constitution in year X (1802) let the Senate rule by acts with the force of law, the sénatus-consultes, in matters the constitution had not foreseen; one, on 2 August 1802, proclaimed Napoleon First Consul for life. Its membership rose to 120, and Bonaparte directly controlled its activity and composition. From January 1803 the sénatoreries ensured its docility: from June 1804, 36 senators received a residence for life and annual revenues of 20,000 to 25,000 francs, so that Berthollet, for example, held the sénatorerie of Montpellier and lived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a>.</p><p>The Constitution of the Year XII (1804) proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-french-empire">First French Empire</a> and made the Senate still more dependent on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>. On 1 January 1806 he gave the senators 54 enemy flags, and Marshal Catherine-Dominique de Pérignon proposed a triumphal arch to his glory. Despite these favours the senators proclaimed his fall in 1814 and summoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a>, who abolished the senate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sénat conservateur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…by him to draft laws, a Tribunate of one hundred to deliberate on them, a Legislative Body of three hundred to vote on them without debate, and a <b>Conservative Senate</b> of sixty members appointed by Bonaparte, which elected the other bodies and later drafted laws itself, the sénatus-consultes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-first-republic">French First Republic · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After Napoleon's abdication in April 1814 the allies and the <b>French Senate</b> restored Louis XVIII, brother of Louis XVI.</blockquote><a href="/state/bourbon-restoration">Bourbon Restoration</a></li></ul></section>
